A quark–anti-quark color state can be viewed as a $3\times3$ matrix $M_i{}^j=q_i\bar q^j$. Every such matrix splits uniquely into its trace and traceless parts, $M=\frac{\operatorname{tr}M}{3}\boldsymbol 1
+\left(M-\frac{\operatorname{tr}M}{3}\boldsymbol 1\right).$ The trace is a single invariant component and the traceless part has eight components. Therefore $\boldsymbol 3\otimes\bar{\boldsymbol 3}
=\boldsymbol 1\oplus\boldsymbol 8.$ Up to normalization, the singlet is $\ket{1}_{q\bar q}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt3}
(\ket{r\bar r}+\ket{g\bar g}+\ket{b\bar b}).$ It cannot rotate into anything else because it is invariant. The other eight linear combinations mix among themselves. They furnish the adjoint representation, the same representation in which the eight generators transform.

